<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Votes for Women</h1><p class="ws-lede">From Wollstonecraft's Vindication to the Swiss vote: the long campaign for women's suffrage.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/votes-for-women">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 5 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Seneca Falls Convention</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Susan B. Anthony</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Representation of the People Act 1918</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Emmeline Pankhurst</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Mary Wollstonecraft</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><p class="ws-how">Write the number of the right dates next to each person. Their pages on the path will help.</p><div class="ws-match"><ol class="ws-ma" type="A"><li>Mary Wollstonecraft</li><li>Susan B. Anthony</li><li>Kate Sheppard</li><li>Emmeline Pankhurst</li></ol><ol class="ws-mb"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1858–1928</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1848–1934</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1820–1906</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1759–1797</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Mary Wollstonecraft</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;In A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792) Wollstonecraft argued that women only appeared inferior because they lacked education, and should be treated as rational beings.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 6, <b>Women's suffrage movement</b>: &#8220;From 1848 to 1971 suffragists used petitions, conventions, lobbying and marches, and won women the vote and the right to stand for election in most of the world's states.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 10, <b>Direct democracy</b>: &#8220;Swiss citizens decide by referendum and in open-air assemblies, and only in 1990 did the Federal Supreme Court order Appenzell Innerrhoden to admit women to its Landsgemeinde.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;From Wollstonecraft's Vindication to the Swiss vote: the long campaign for women's suffrage.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/votes-for-women. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Votes for Women</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Mary Wollstonecraft <span>born 1759 CE</span></li><li>Susan B. Anthony <span>born 1820 CE</span></li><li>Seneca Falls Convention <span>1848</span></li><li>Emmeline Pankhurst <span>born 1858 CE</span></li><li>Representation of the People Act 1918 <span>1918</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><ol class="ws-key-list" type="A"><li>Mary Wollstonecraft <span>4: 1759–1797</span></li><li>Susan B. Anthony <span>3: 1820–1906</span></li><li>Kate Sheppard <span>2: 1848–1934</span></li><li>Emmeline Pankhurst <span>1: 1858–1928</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
